---
layout: article
title: Updates and migrations
description: Keep your self-hosted Appwrite instance up-to-date. Learn how to perform updates, manage versions, and ensure your self-hosted Appwrite stays current.
---

To upgrade your Appwrite server from an older version, you should use the Appwrite migration tool *after you have installed the new version*. The migration tool will adjust your Appwrite data to the new version's structure to make sure your Appwrite data is compatible with any internal changes.

You can upgrade to a newer patch version without running the migration unless the [release notes](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases) indicate a migration is required. For example, you can upgrade from [`1.6.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.6.0) to [`1.6.1`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.6.1) without running the migrate command, but upgrading from `1.6.0` to `1.6.2` or later will require the migrate command because [`1.6.2`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.6.2) requires a migration.

If you're trying to migrate to a newer minor version, you should upgrade to each minor version's latest patch. For example, if you're upgrading from `1.5.1` to `1.7.4` you should upgrade to:

1. `1.5.11`
1. `1.6.2`
1. `1.7.4`

`1.9` is the exception: [`1.9.6`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.9.6) added a new migration in a patch release, and each migration only runs its own changes. From `1.8.x` or earlier, upgrade to `1.9.5` and run the migration before moving to `1.9.10`, or the `1.9` migration is skipped. For example, from `1.8.1` to `2.7.1` you should upgrade to:

1. `1.9.5`
1. `1.9.10`
1. `2.7.1`

Recent versions share a migration and can skip minor versions. The [latest upgrade section](#upgrading-to-latest) names the oldest version that upgrades to the current release in one step. From an older version, upgrade to that version first.

Before upgrading, be sure to:

1. [back up your server](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/production/backups) data before running the migration
1. review the [changelog](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases) for any breaking changes
1. test the migration process on a non-production instance to make sure your application is working well

# Upgrading to 2.7.1 {% #upgrading-to-latest %}

Back up your data first.

2.7.1 is a security release. It updates the Open Runtimes executor from `1.1.2` to `1.3.1`. The old executor followed symlinks that a function or site planted in its logs, build output or build cache, so a deployment could read files from the executor container, including `_APP_EXECUTOR_SECRET`. With that secret, code in a function can call the executor directly, and the executor controls Docker on the host. If anyone you don't fully trust can deploy functions or sites on your instance, upgrade and then rotate `_APP_EXECUTOR_SECRET`.

From `2.7.0`, the upgrade only replaces the images. 2.7.1 has no schema changes and keeps the same 1.2.138 Console, so the migration has nothing to change.

Appwrite 2.7.1 upgrades from `1.9.6` or any later version. Run the upgrade command with the `2.7.1` tag, then run the migration.

```sh
docker run -it --rm \
    --publish 20080:20080 \
    --volume /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
    --volume "$(pwd)"/appwrite:/usr/src/code/appwrite:rw \
    --entrypoint="upgrade" \
    appwrite/appwrite:2.7.1
```

Then run the migration from the `appwrite` directory.

```sh
cd appwrite/
docker compose exec appwrite migrate
```

If you maintain your own `docker-compose.yml`, set the `openruntimes-executor` image to `openruntimes/executor:1.3.1` and run `docker compose up -d`.

To rotate the executor secret, set a new `_APP_EXECUTOR_SECRET` in `.env` and run `docker compose up -d`. This recreates the API, the workers and the executor together, so nothing needs the old value.

From `2.6.x` or earlier, the V25 migration is idempotent and adds:

- `prefs` on projects; `alertsUnsubscribed` on users; the GeoIP attributes on sessions; and `folder` with its index on buckets created before 2.0.0.
- `type` on transactions, backfilled to `legacy` for existing ones.
- `topicInternalId` indexes on the push ledger. Ledger entries are now bound to their topic, so entries written before the upgrade have no `topicInternalId` and are never replayed. Push messages a device had not yet received over MQTT when you upgrade are not delivered when it reconnects; the age-based prune removes those entries. New messages replay as before.
- The `transfers`, `monitors`, `monitorAlerts` and `reviews` collections, used by API features that have no self-hosted Console screen yet.

If you're upgrading from `2.6.x` or earlier, also check these, which come from 2.7.0:

- **Server key**: nothing to change. Keep `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_V1`. To rotate it later, recreate the containers after each configuration change with `docker compose up -d` (`docker compose restart` keeps the old environment): set `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_V2` in `.env` and run `docker compose up -d`; set `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_CURRENT=2` and run `docker compose up -d`; run `docker compose exec appwrite encryption` (a dry run that counts what's left), then `docker compose exec appwrite encryption --commit=true` (resume with `--after=<projectId>` if it stops); then set `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_OLDEST=2` and run `docker compose up -d` to stop accepting V1 tokens. JWTs and resource tokens signed with V1 stop working at that point. Files already encrypted in storage are not re-encrypted, so keep `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_V1` configured.
- **Realtime and MQTT clients**: a connection over 100 subscriptions, channels or queries now gets an error, and a user's 33rd connection on one worker is closed. Raise the realtime limits with the variables above if your app needs more.
- **Bulk writes**: bulk update, upsert and delete from client sessions now return 401. They need an API key or a console user.
- **API keys**: a key that creates a migration now needs the write scope of every resource it imports, and a key that configures a function or site can only add scopes it holds itself.
- **MFA**: turning MFA off now needs a recent MFA challenge, and a TOTP code can be used once.
- **PostgreSQL**: the bundled PostgreSQL starts with `max_connections=500`. Set `_APP_DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS` lower on small hosts.
- **Functions domain**: if you changed `_APP_DOMAIN_FUNCTIONS`, list the old domain in `_APP_DOMAIN_FUNCTIONS_FALLBACK` to keep serving preview URLs on it.
- **Maintenance**: `_APP_MAINTENANCE_INTERVAL` should be a multiple of 300 seconds. The default, 86400, is.
- **New optional variables**: `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_V2`, `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_CURRENT`, `_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_OLDEST`, `_APP_LIMIT_REALTIME_SUBSCRIPTIONS`, `_APP_LIMIT_REALTIME_CHANNELS`, `_APP_LIMIT_REALTIME_QUERIES`, `_APP_DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS` and `_APP_DOMAIN_FUNCTIONS_FALLBACK`.

If you skip releases, also follow the upgrade notes of each release you skip: [`2.0.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.0.0), [`2.1.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.1.0), [`2.2.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.2.0), [`2.3.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.3.0), [`2.4.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.4.0), [`2.5.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.5.0), [`2.6.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.6.0), [`2.6.1`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.6.1), [`2.6.2`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.6.2), [`2.7.0`](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.7.0).

# Upgrading within the 1.9 line {% #upgrading-1-9 %}

If you are staying on Appwrite 1.9, upgrade to `1.9.10`. It fixes the Brotli decoder vulnerability [CVE-2026-111497](https://github.com/google/brotli/releases/tag/v1.2.1) and backports security fixes from 2.6.2. It doesn't change the database schema. Some fixes tighten defaults: project SMTP, messaging SMTP providers and migration sources that point at a private or reserved address now need that address in `_APP_ALLOWED_INTERNAL_ADDRESSES`, which 1.9.10 adds to the 1.9 line. Read the [1.9.10 release notes](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.9.10) before upgrading.

From `1.9.8` or earlier, `1.9.10` also includes the fixes of each release you skip: `1.9.9` moves the runtime to PHP 8.5.12, and `1.9.8` refreshes outbound TLS trust after the eMudhra certificate compromise and applies the SVG sanitizer fixes.

Back up your database and persistent storage. From the parent directory of your installation, run:

```sh
docker run -it --rm \
    --publish 20080:20080 \
    --volume /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
    --volume "$(pwd)"/appwrite:/usr/src/code/appwrite:rw \
    --entrypoint="upgrade" \
    appwrite/appwrite:1.9.10
cd appwrite/
docker compose exec appwrite migrate
```

Outbound HTTPS endpoints that still present a compromised certificate chain must replace their certificate chain. See the [1.9.10](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.9.10), [1.9.9](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.9.9) and [1.9.8](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/1.9.8) release notes for details.

# Installing the next version {% #install-next-version %}

The first step is to install the latest version of Appwrite. Head to the directory where you ran your previous Appwrite install command.

```text
parent_directory <= you run the command in this directory
└── appwrite
    └── docker-compose.yml
```

The parent directory is where you will find the appwrite directory, inside which there are `docker-compose.yml` and `.env` files.

{% info title="Parent directory naming" %}
Your Appwrite installation's parent directory name is expected to be `appwrite`. Changing the directory name will result in mismatched Docker project names.
{% /info %}

{% info title="Choose an image tag" %}
Replace `<APPWRITE_VERSION>` below with the specific Appwrite image tag you intend to run (for example, `2.7.1`). Avoid using `latest` in production.
{% /info %}

## Unix

```sh
docker run -it --rm \
    --publish 20080:20080 \
    --volume /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
    --volume "$(pwd)"/appwrite:/usr/src/code/appwrite:rw \
    --entrypoint="upgrade" \
    appwrite/appwrite:<APPWRITE_VERSION>
```

## CMD

```cmd
docker run -it --rm ^
    --publish 20080:20080 ^
    --volume //var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock ^
    --volume "%cd%"/appwrite:/usr/src/code/appwrite:rw ^
    --entrypoint="upgrade" ^
    appwrite/appwrite:<APPWRITE_VERSION>
```

## PowerShell

```powershell
docker run -it --rm `
    --publish 20080:20080 `
    --volume /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock `
    --volume ${pwd}/appwrite:/usr/src/code/appwrite:rw `
    --entrypoint="upgrade" `
    appwrite/appwrite:<APPWRITE_VERSION>
```

This will pull the `docker-compose.yml` for the selected version/tag and perform the upgrade steps.
Once the setup completes, verify that you have the latest version of Appwrite.

```sh
docker ps | grep appwrite/appwrite
```

Verify that the `STATUS` doesn't have any errors and all the `appwrite/appwrite` containers have the same version.

# Running the migration {% #running-the-migration %}

We can now start the migration. Navigate to the `appwrite` directory where your `docker-compose.yml` is present and run the following command.

```sh
cd appwrite/
docker compose exec appwrite migrate
```

The data migration can take longer depending on the amount of data your Appwrite instance contains. The Appwrite migration command uses multi-threading to speed up the process, meaning that adding more CPU cores can help speed up the process.

Once the migration process has been completed successfully, you're all set to use the latest version of Appwrite!

